(vector (map (:adr/authoritative_for (vector "70-tools/bmc/src/gftd/traffic.cljc" "70-tools/bmc/collect.cljs channel-measurement" "gftdcojp/network-isekai public/play.html itonami credit link" "gftdcojp/cloud-itonami public/index.html isekai demo link")), (:adr/topic "itonami-isekai-channel-traffic-integration"), (:adr/related (vector "adr-2607172700" "adr-2607172800" "adr-2607172900" "adr-2607021800")), (:adr/status "accepted"), (:adr/id "adr-2607231800-itonami-isekai-channel-traffic-integration"), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:adr/last_verified "2026-07-23"), (:adr/body "# ADR-2607231800: cloud-itonami <-> network-isekai channel-measurement + growth-loop integration\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-23\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki (agent task: implement the cloud-itonami <-> network-isekai channel-measurement integration design)\n**Related**: ADR-2607172700 (BMC LLM advisor murakumo wiring) / ADR-2607172800 (LLM SSE streaming) / ADR-2607172900 (itonami qwen3.6 standing runner) / ADR-2607021800 (collect.cljs) / agent/isekai-channel-bot-filter (PR #1157, bot-probe filter this ADR's traffic module supersedes-by-extraction)\n\n## Context\n\nnetwork-isekai's channel-measurement top-paths were polluted by bot/vulnerability-scanner probe requests answered 2xx/3xx by Cloudflare Pages' unmatched-path fallback (evidence: 90-docs/business/canvas-ledger.edn :network-isekai.channels ticks 2026-07-09..22 show /mailer.php /wp.php /a1.php /1996.php /biufile.php /w3lls.php /inputs.php /wp-lvminl.php /wp-admin/css/colors/index.php /.git /config/config.php /config/nexmo.php /Dockerfile /package-updates/yum.cgi //abcd.php //avcqlevnbk.php as \"top pages\"). A prior branch (agent/isekai-channel-bot-filter, PR #1157, still open at the time this ADR was written) fixed this inline in 70-tools/bmc/collect.cljs via probe-path-re / channel-allowlist / classify-path. This ADR's design additionally asks to (a) generalize the itonami growth-loop *scheduler* (not the already-shared `gftd react tick --product <p>` loop mechanism) to also cover network-isekai, (b) evaluate an itonami<->isekai cross-referral, and (c) extract the bot-probe classification into a shared library module rather than leaving it inlined only in collect.cljs.\n\n## Decision\n\n### (c) Unify measurement/analytics — shared gftd.traffic module\n\n`classify-path` / `probe-path-re` / `channel-allowlist` are extracted out of collect.cljs into a new pure module `70-tools/bmc/src/gftd/traffic.cljc` (matching the existing gftd.canvas/gate/funnel/react/score module conventions). collect.cljs now `require`s gftd.traffic and threads the product keyword into `zone-top-paths`, so any product's collector can opt in via `channel-allowlist` (currently :network-isekai and :cloud-itonami). This is a **reimplementation** of PR #1157's inline logic in a shared-module shape — branched from origin/main independently (per task instructions, landing order between this and #1157 was uncertain) rather than building on top of #1157's branch. The two PRs will conflict; recommended resolution is to close #1157 in favor of this one (or rebase this one on top of #1157 if #1157 lands first — either way, only one of the two classify-path implementations should land, not both). `collect.cljs` requires `70-tools/bmc/src` on the nbb classpath; the superproject-root `nbb.edn` already provides this when nbb is invoked from the repo root, so no operational change is needed for the common case — README.md documents the explicit `--classpath` fallback for any invocation from a different cwd (e.g. a LaunchAgent `WorkingDirectory` elsewhere). Test coverage added in `70-tools/bmc/test/gftd/bmc_test.cljc` using the real observed bot-probe paths from canvas-ledger.edn.\n\n### (a) Share the growth-loop automation — scheduler-level finding\n\nThe loop mechanism (`gftd react tick --product <p>`) is already generic/shared infrastructure used by all 11 gftdcojp products including network-isekai — confirmed via `bmc-business-operate-daily` (a claude.ai RemoteTrigger routine, cron `0 22 * * *`) whose prompt already iterates network-isekai in its 11-product `--product` loop list. No new sharing is needed at the loop-mechanism level.\n\nWhat is itonami-*specific* is a second, higher-frequency (hourly) scheduler: `itonami-react-growth-hourly` (a claude.ai RemoteTrigger routine, cron `30 * * * *`, id trig_01Cc9u7vATaKtvnPRn99ij6x). **Correction to this task's design brief**: the brief, quoting ADR-2607172900, assumed this routine is \"invisible to the current session's RemoteTrigger list\" and its scope unconfirmable. That assumption no longer holds — this session's RemoteTrigger list call *does* surface it, and its actual prompt is directly readable: it targets **cloud-itonami only** (not network-isekai), and its prompt still invokes the retired `bb 70-tools/bmc/collect.bb` / `70-tools/bmc/bin/gftd react loop --max-ticks 4` (pre-nbb-migration syntax, matching the stale-bb note already in CLAUDE.md's BMC section). This ADR does **not** silently rewrite this routine's live prompt (broadening its scope and/or fixing bb->nbb) as part of implementing the design — modifying a live scheduled cloud routine that governs production PR-generation for the whole portfolio is a larger, separate decision than this task's file-level scope, and the routine's own `bb`->`nbb` staleness is a pre-existing, separately-tracked issue (CLAUDE.md). Recorded here as an **owner-confirm follow-up**: (1) should `itonami-react-growth-hourly` be broadened to also tick network-isekai hourly (on top of its existing daily coverage via `bmc-business-operate-daily`), and (2) its `bb`->`nbb` staleness should be fixed regardless. The macOS LaunchAgent `com.gftd.itonami-qwen36-tick` + `~/.gftd/run-itonami-qwen36-tick.cljs` described in ADR-2607172900 as \"registered\" was **not found on this machine at implementation time** (no matching file under ~/.gftd, no matching LaunchAgent under ~/Library/LaunchAgents, not in `launchctl list`) — either already retired, ephemeral, or living on a different host/session than this one; this ADR does not fabricate its presence or blindly recreate it without owner confirmation of its current status.\n\n### (b) Cross-referral — audience-honest, narrow, one direction built\n\nisekai is a public UGC/creator/player game platform (B2C/prosumer); itonami is a B2B SMB ops/compliance SaaS (buyer = SMB operator/CFO/legal/investment fund). There is essentially zero end-user overlap — a symmetric homepage-to-homepage cross-link was evaluated and rejected as an audience mismatch (would read as off-brand noise on both sides). A real, narrower, already-shipped integration point exists instead: cloud-itonami's ISIC vertical blueprint actors are already published as playable \"governor approval loop as a depot round\" demo games on network-isekai under `/itonami/<vertical>` (`public/games/itonami/{freight-dispatch,building-site,care-coordination,market-garden,plumbing-rounds,retail-shift,freight-run,home-nursing-rounds}/*`, confirmed present on network-isekai's `origin/main`), routed via the existing `/itonami/* -> /play` `public/_redirects` rule.\n\nTwo concrete, scoped, non-symmetric changes were built:\n1. **network-isekai -> itonami.cloud** (gftdcojp/network-isekai PR #271): a low-key, hidden-by-default credit-line pill (\"built by cloud-itonami — try the real product\") on the game player, shown only when the resolved game id starts with `itonami/` (i.e. only on these 8 vertical-demo games, invisible for every `gftd/*` game and in `?embed=1` mode). Links to `https://itonami.cloud/?utm_source=isekai&utm_medium=demo-game&utm_campaign=<vertical>`.\n2. **cloud-itonami -> isekai demo hub** (gftdcojp/cloud-itonami PR #477): a single outbound \"Vertical demos (isekai)\" button on the itonami cockpit's action bar, linking to `https://isekai.network/itonami/cloud-itonami?utm_source=itonami&utm_medium=product-site&utm_campaign=vertical-demo-hub`. This is the more defensible direction (a B2B prospect already on the cockpit is shown a playable demo of the governed workflow as sales-enablement content) and is built as a single link, not a generic promotional section.\n\nNeither change is site-wide, neither is a push banner, and no change assumes isekai's player base is a lead pool for itonami or vice versa.\n\n## Important correction found during implementation\n\nThe design brief this ADR implements assumed a richer `/{org}/{repo}` \"project workspace\" page already existed in production for `/itonami/cloud-itonami` (`public/projects/itonami/cloud-itonami/project.edn`, `src/isekai/project.cljs`, a `namespace-projects` route table in `src/isekai/routes.cljc`). **That infrastructure is not present on network-isekai's `origin/main`** as of this ADR (verified via a fresh worktree checkout of `origin/main`: no `public/projects/` directory, no `src/isekai/project.cljs`, no `namespace-projects` table; `public/_redirects` routes `/itonami/*` directly to `/play`, not `/project`). This content exists only in an unmerged local WIP checkout on the implementer's machine (branch `rescue/full-wip-20260718`, 389 commits behind and diverged from current `origin/main`) — i.e. it was never actually shipped to production isekai.network, contrary to what the design brief's \"confirmed files\" framing assumed. This ADR's actual isekai-side change (PR #271) targets the real current production surface for these games instead (`public/play.html`, via the `/itonami/* -> /play` redirect that IS live on `origin/main`), and does not attempt to land the large diverged rescue branch (out of scope, high risk, a separate cleanup task).\n\n## Correction before merge (pre-merge review)\n\nThe originally-shipped `channel-allowlist` also included a `:cloud-itonami` entry, carried over unchanged from PR #1157's inline version. Pre-merge review caught that this entry was actively harmful, not merely unrefined: cloud-itonami does not have network-isekai's \"Cloudflare Pages answers unmatched paths as 2xx\" problem (its status-only split already reports a clean probe percentage, per this ADR's own Context section) and the entry's own route list shipped with a \"refine against itonami.cloud's actual route map before relying on this\" caveat that was never actually done — so registering it narrowed cloud-itonami's top-paths measurement to 4 unverified routes for zero corrective benefit, immediately upon merge. Removed the `:cloud-itonami` entry from `channel-allowlist` (`70-tools/bmc/src/gftd/traffic.cljc`) and updated the corresponding test in `70-tools/bmc/test/gftd/bmc_test.cljc` accordingly; cloud-itonami now falls back to its pre-existing (already-working) status-only behavior, same as every other product without an allowlist entry. If cloud-itonami's route map is verified later, an entry can be reinstated then.\n\n## Consequences / follow-ups\n\n- Two open PRs need reconciliation: agent/isekai-channel-bot-filter (#1157, inline fix) vs this ADR's gftd.traffic extraction (same fix, shared-module shape) — only one should land.\n- Owner-confirm: broaden `itonami-react-growth-hourly` to also cover network-isekai hourly, and fix its stale `bb`->`nbb` invocation, or leave network-isekai on its existing daily-only coverage via `bmc-business-operate-daily`.\n- Owner-confirm: current status of the `com.gftd.itonami-qwen36-tick` LaunchAgent / `~/.gftd/run-itonami-qwen36-tick.cljs` described in ADR-2607172900 — not found on this machine; if it should exist, it needs to be (re)created, generalized to a `--product` loop covering both cloud-itonami and network-isekai per the original design.\n- The unmerged `rescue/full-wip-20260718` branch on the local network-isekai checkout (389 commits behind/diverged from origin/main) is a separate, larger cleanup — not addressed by this ADR; flagged here for visibility since it contains real production-adjacent WIP (the `/{org}/{repo}` project-workspace feature).\n- gftdcojp/network-isekai PR #271 and gftdcojp/cloud-itonami PR #477 are both open at the time of this ADR and need normal review/merge in their own repos.\n"), (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki")), (:adr/date "2026-07-23"), (:db/id -1), (:adr/authoritative true), (:adr/title "ADR-2607231800: cloud-itonami <-> network-isekai channel-measurement + growth-loop integration"), (:adr/source-format "edn")))
